@startuml
title Resolution context — the ambient state every node is evaluated against

skinparam linetype ortho

/'
  Every evaluator (INumber, ICondition at the top level, ITarget, IEffect,
  IAttempt, IAction) takes a ResolutionContext instead of a bare Battle.
'/

class ResolutionContext <<record>> {
    - battle: Battle
    - controller: Side
    /' The card whose text is being resolved. `Self` and `SelfSide` resolve
       through this, which is how a Tool or Ability trigger can mean
       "the Pokemon I am attached to". '/
    - source: PokemonInPlay
    /' Present only while a trigger is resolving; carries the payload of the
       event that fired it. '/
    - event: Optional<GameEvent>
    - scope: ResolutionScope

    + withSource(PokemonInPlay): ResolutionContext
    + withEvent(GameEvent): ResolutionContext
    + opponentOf(Side): Side
    + rng(): RandomSource
    + choose(Decision<T>): T
}

/'
  Deliberately mutable, and the only mutable thing reachable from an otherwise
  immutable context. It is the scratchpad for one resolution: a coin flip
  performed by an IFlipStrategy has to be readable by the INumber that follows
  it in the same Attempt.
'/
class ResolutionScope {
    - lastFlip: Optional<FlipResult>
    - damageDealtThisResolution: int

    + recordFlip(FlipResult): void
}

class FlipResult <<record>> {
    - results: List<boolean>

    + heads(): int
    + tails(): int
    + allHeads(): boolean
    + lastWasHeads(): boolean
}

/'
  Seeded so that a whole game replays identically. Everything random goes
  through here: FlipN, FlipUntilTails, shuffleDeck, the *Random targets,
  SpreadDamage distribution, and RandomPlayer's decisions.
'/
interface RandomSource {
    + nextInt(int bound): int
    + nextBoolean(): boolean
    + shuffle(List<?>): void
}

class SeededRandom implements RandomSource {
    - seed: long
}

ResolutionContext *-- ResolutionScope
ResolutionScope o-- FlipResult
ResolutionContext ..> Battle
ResolutionContext ..> Side
ResolutionContext ..> PokemonInPlay
ResolutionContext ..> GameEvent
Battle *-- RandomSource

note right of ResolutionContext
  Why not just pass Battle and keep a context stack on it?
  Because that recreates exactly the hidden-global problem
  `Battle.previousHeads` already had: a flip performed deep
  inside one attack would be visible to unrelated code, and
  nested resolution (a trigger firing mid-attack) would have
  to remember to push and pop correctly. Threading an
  immutable context makes nesting free and correct.
end note

@enduml
